=== Quantization of spin wave: magnon
<quantization-of-spin-wave-magnon>


In a quantum-mechanical picture, the SW corresponds to the quasi-particle called magnon. It is a boson obeying Bose-Einstein statistics, and, in the absence of fields and anisotropy, corresponds to a Goldstone boson. Magnons were originally proposed by Bloch in a spin-$1 \/ 2$ system [76] to explain the reduction of magnetization with increasing temperature, i.e., the celebrated $T^(3 \/ 2)$ law. Extension to the higher spin case is due to Möller Holstein and Primakoff quantized the magnetic excitation based on the bosonic creation and annihilation operators and derived the Hamiltonian of magnons with both quadratic and higher-order terms, which give the non-interacting magnon behavior and interaction between magnons, respectively. This approach has become a standard technique to study the quantum behavior of magnons. Magnon theory was further developed by Dyson [79] to consider the influence of magnon interactions on the low temperature behavior of thermodynamic quantities. Here we briefly review the Holstein-Primakoff (HP) approach. To describe a spin-$S$ state in quantum mechanics [80], we consider the simultaneous eigenstates of the spin angular momentum operator $hat(upright(bold(S)))^2$ and its component along the quantization axis ($upright(bold(e))_z$) $hat(S)_z$ as $ hat(upright(bold(S)))^2 | S, m chevron.r = S ( S + 1 ) | S, m chevron.r, quad hat(S)_z | S, m chevron.r = m | S, m chevron.r, quad m = -S, -S + 1, dots., S - 1, S. $ However, with this approach it is difficult to treat the interaction between many spins and magnons. To overcome this difficulty, Holstein and Primakoff noticed that [78] the possible values of $m$ can be rewritten as $m equiv S - n$, where the new quantum number $n$ takes only non-negative values $n = 0, 1, 2, dots., 2S$, regardless of whether one deals with fermions (half-integer $S$) or bosons (integer $S$). Transforming to this new basis $| S, n chevron.r$, we have $hat(S)_z | S, n chevron.r = ( S - n ) | S, n chevron.r$. In comparison with the quantization of the harmonic oscillator $hat(a)^dagger hat(a) | n chevron.r = n | n chevron.r$, we immediately have the second-quantization form of $hat(S)_z$, i.e., $hat(S)_z = S - hat(a)^dagger hat(a)$, where $hat(a)$ ($hat(a)^dagger$) is the annihilation (creation) operator of a spin quantum or a magnon. To make the new representation complete, we need to find a representation of $hat(S)_x$ and $hat(S)_y$ by preserving the commutation relation for angular momentum $[ hat(S)_i, hat(S)_j ] = i epsilon.alt_(i j k) hat(S)_k$, where $epsilon.alt_(i j k)$ is the Levi-Civita symbol. To this end, we recall the spin raising and lowering operators defined as, $hat(S)^plus.minus = hat(S)_x plus.minus i hat(S)_y$. Based on the quantum theory of angular momentum, we have $ hat(S)^(+) | S, n chevron.r = sqrt(2 S - ( n - 1 )) sqrt(n) | S, n - 1 chevron.r = sqrt(2 S - hat(a)^dagger hat(a)) hat(a) | S, n chevron.r . $ We therefore obtain $hat(S)^(+) = sqrt(2 S - hat(a)^dagger hat(a)) hat(a)$ and, similarly, $hat(S)^(-) = hat(a)^dagger sqrt(2 S - hat(a)^dagger hat(a))$, which allows us to express the excitation of a single spin $S$ in terms of magnon creation and annihilation operators. A real magnet involves many interacting spins and is described by the general Hamiltonian [81,82] 
$
hat(cal(H)) = - J sum_(chevron.l i, j chevron.r) hat(upright(bold(S)))_i dot.op hat(upright(bold(S)))_j + hat(cal(H))_"an" - mu_0 g_e mu_B sum_i upright(bold(H))_i dot.op hat(upright(bold(S)))_i - frac(mu_0 ( g_e mu_B )^2, 4 pi) sum_(i < j) frac(3 ( hat(upright(bold(S)))_i dot.op upright(bold(e))_(i j) ) ( hat(upright(bold(S)))_j dot.op upright(bold(e))_(i j) ) - hat(upright(bold(S)))_i dot.op hat(upright(bold(S)))_j, | upright(bold(r))_(i j) |^3)
$ <generalHam>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("generalHam")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $J$ is the exchange coefficient and the first sum is over nearest neighbouring spins, $upright(bold(H))_i$ is the external magnetic field on the $i$-th spin, $mu_0$ is vacuum magnetic permeability, $g_e$ is the Landé $g$-factor, $mu_B$ is Bohr magneton, $upright(bold(r))_(i j)$ is the vector between the $i$-th and $j$-th spin, and $upright(bold(e))_(i j)$ is the unit direction vector connecting sites $i$ and $j$. On the right hand side of $hat(cal(H))$, the first term is the exchange coupling, the second term is the magnetocrystalline anisotropy that prefers spins to align in particular directions, the third term is the Zeeman energy, and the last term is the long-range dipolar interaction. Depending on the competition of these interactions, the ground state of the system may be a collinear magnetic state, such as a homogeneous magnetic domain, or a noncollinear magnetic texture, such as a domain wall, vortex, skyrmion, etc [83,84,85,86,87]. To consider the magnon excitations above these magnetic states, one usually performs a HP transformation on the ground state of the system and derives the effective Hamiltonian for magnons as [88,89,90] $ hat(cal(H)) = hat(cal(H))_0 + hat(cal(H))^( 2 ) + hat(cal(H))^( 3 ) + hat(cal(H))^( 4 ) + dots. $ where $hat(cal(H))_0$ is ground state energy, $hat(cal(H))^( 2 ), hat(cal(H))^( 3 ), hat(cal(H))^( 4 )$ denote the two-magnon processes contributed by exchange field, external field, anisotropy field and dipolar interactions, three-magnon processes from dipolar interactions, and four magnon processes from exchange and dipolar interactions, respectively. If the magnon density of the system is very low, which typically holds for weak perturbations well below the Curie temperature, the three- and four-magnon processes can be neglected. It is then sufficient to diagonalize the quadratic component $hat(cal(H))^( 2 )$ to derive the eigenspectrum. Note that this approach works for both collinear and noncollinear magnetic structures


For sufficiently large $S$, the quantum approach should give an identical prediction of the low energy SW dispersion as starting from the LLG equation, because the diagonalization procedure of the Hamiltonian corresponds to solving the eigenvalues of the dynamic matrix of the quantum Heisenberg equation, which is equivalent to solving the linearized LLG equation in the absence of damping. As an example, let us consider a soft magnetic film with magnetization aligned along the in-plane field $upright(bold(H)) = H upright(bold(e))_z$. Hamiltonian of the system is 
$
hat(cal(H)) = - J sum_(chevron.l i, j chevron.r) hat(upright(bold(S)))_i dot.op hat(upright(bold(S)))_j + K_x sum_i (hat(S)_(i, x))^2 - K_z sum_i (hat(S)_(i, z))^2 - mu_0 g_e mu_B H sum_i hat(S)_(i, z)
$ <biaxialHam>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("biaxialHam")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $K_x$ and $K_z$ are anisotropies along the $x$ and $z$ directions, respectively, with $K_x > 0, K_z > 0$. The classical ground state of the system is $chevron.l hat(upright(bold(S))) chevron.r = S upright(bold(e))_z$. By keeping the linear terms of the HP transformation of the spin operators along this ground state, i.e., 
$
hat(S)_(i, z) = S - hat(a)_i^dagger hat(a)_i, quad hat(S)_(i, x) = sqrt(2 S) \/ 2 ( hat(a)_i + hat(a)_i^dagger ), quad hat(S)_(i, y) = frac(sqrt(2 S), 2 i) ( hat(a)_i - hat(a)_i^dagger )
$ <HPtransform>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("HPtransform")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $hat(a)_i$ ($hat(a)_i^dagger$) is the magnon annihilation (creation) operator on the $i -$th spin site, the effective Hamiltonian of magnon excitations to the quadratic terms can be written as 
$
cal(hat(H))^( 2 ) = - J S sum_(chevron.l i, j chevron.r) ( hat(a)_i hat(a)_j^dagger + h . c . ) + sum_i mu_i hat(a)_i^dagger hat(a)_i + frac(K_x S, 2) sum_i ( hat(a)_i hat(a)_i + h . c . )
$ <hamsse>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("hamsse")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $mu_i = 2 Z J S + mu_0 g_e mu_B H + 2 K_z S + K_x S$ with $Z$ being the coordination number. By switching to Fourier space and diagonalizing the Hamiltonian, we obtain the bosonic Hamiltonian of the system as 
$
cal(hat(H))^( 2 ) = c o n s t . + sum_k omega_k hat(a)_(upright(bold(k)))^dagger hat(a)_(upright(bold(k))) .
$ <sq:eq:Hmag>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:Hmag")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] Here the eigenfrequency is $ omega_k = sqrt([ 2 J S ( k d )^2 + mu_0 g_e mu_B H + 2 K_x S ] [ 2 J S ( k d )^2 + mu_0 g_e mu_B H + 2 K_z S + 2 K_x S ])   $ in the long wavelength limit with $d$ the lattice constant. The same dispersion is obtained employing the LLG phenomenology.


 anisotropy may substantially alter the nature of the magnonic eigenmodes in a ferromagnet leading to squeezing, as discussed in @sec_squeezedmagnon below. Here and in the next section, we examine magnons in an isotropic ferromagnet @sec:cohstate. The corresponding considerations in an anisotropic ferromagnet will be addressed in @sec_squeezedmagnon. For a magnetic sphere with negligible magnetocrystalline anisotropy ($K_x = K_z = 0$), i.e., an istrotropic magnet, the resonance frequency $omega_k = mu_0 g_e mu_B H + 2 J S ( k d )^2$. Employing $hat(S)_(i, z) = S - hat(a)_i^dagger hat(a)_i$ from the HP approach, the total spin $z$-component in an eigenstate is evaluated as 
$
⟨hat(cal(S))_z⟩ equiv ⟨sum_i hat(S)_(i, z)⟩ & = N S - sum_(upright(bold(k))) ⟨hat(a)_(upright(bold(k)))^dagger hat(a)_(upright(bold(k)))⟩ = N S - sum_(upright(bold(k))) n_(upright(bold(k)))
$ <sq:eq:spin1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:spin1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $N$ is the total number of lattice sites and $n_(upright(bold(k)))$ is the number of $upright(bold(k))$ magnons. #ref(<sq:eq:spin1>, supplement: none) shows that excitation of each magnon alters the total spin by unity, which is why each magnon carries one unit ($ℏ$) of spin angular momentum along $upright(bold(e))_z$ Within this linear approximation [#ref(<HPtransform>, supplement: none)], magnons are non-interacting bosonic modes. Hence, without loss of generality, we may focus on the $upright(bold(k)) = bold(0)$ mode addressed in typical ferromagnetic resonance experiments 
$
hat(cal(H)) & = omega_r hat(a)^dagger hat(a)
$ <sq:eq:H1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:H1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $omega_r equiv omega_(upright(bold(k)) arrow.r bold(0))$ and we have further dropped the subscript $bold(0)$ of the magnon ladder operators for convenience.


=== Coherent states and classical dynamics
<sec:cohstate>
Now we wish to relate the magnonic quantum picture to the phenomenological LL description, considering the $upright(bold(k)) = bold(0)$ mode in an isotropic ferromagnet [#ref(<sq:eq:H1>, supplement: none)]. Employing #ref(<HPtransform>, supplement: none) and the properties of the number states, we evaluate the expectation value 
$
⟨hat(cal(S))_x⟩ & equiv ⟨sum_i hat(S)_(i, x)⟩ = sqrt(frac(N S, 2)) ⟨hat(a) + hat(a)^dagger⟩ = 0
$ <sq:eq:expect1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:expect1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] in any eigenstate of the Hamiltonian #ref(<sq:eq:H1>, supplement: none). The expectation value of $hat(cal(S))_y equiv ⟨sum_i hat(S)_(i, y)⟩$ vanishes similarly. This is in contrast with typical experiments which find a finite value for the spin's $x$ and $y$ components. What has gone wrong? The equivalent question for harmonic oscillators bothered Schrödinger and led him to find the states that reproduce the classically observed dynamics [96]. These are called coherent states [97,98,99] and are eigenstates of the annihilation operator $ hat(a) |beta⟩ & = beta |beta⟩   $ where $beta$ is a complex number. Using this property and #ref(<sq:eq:expect1>, supplement: none), it is seen directly that the expectation values of $hat(cal(S))_(x, y)$ in a coherent state do not vanish coherent state can further be expressed in terms of Fock states [100] $|n⟩$ 
$
|beta⟩ & = sum_n exp(- frac(| beta |^2, 2)) beta^n \/ sqrt(n !) |n⟩
$ <sq:eq:alphanum>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:alphanum")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] from which we see that a coherent state has a Poissonian distribution of the magnon number $n$. We further evaluate the mean $macron(n)$ and uncertainty $Delta n$ of magnon number 
$
bar(n) equiv ⟨beta | hat(n) | beta⟩ equiv ⟨hat(n)⟩ = |beta |^2, quad ( Delta n )^2 equiv ⟨hat(n)^2⟩ - (⟨hat(n)⟩ )^2 = |beta |^2, quad ⇒ frac(Delta n, bar(n)) = frac(1, |beta |) = frac(1, sqrt(bar(n))).
$ <sq:eq:cohprop>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:cohprop")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] properties above clarify the classical-quantum correspondence that we have sought. It is not sufficient to consider a high magnon number alone, as one might guess at first, to recover classical dynamics from the quantum picture. This is because the expectation values of $hat(cal(S))_(x, y)$ in any number state vanish [#ref(<sq:eq:expect1>, supplement: none)]. On the other hand, the coherent state which is a superposition of number states yields a finite expectation value [97,98,99]. Furthermore, in the limit of a large average magnon number $macron(n)$, the relative fluctuations become small [#ref(<sq:eq:cohprop>, supplement: none)] giving rise to the classical picture of a spin precession with (nearly) constant amplitude [99,101]


Let us take one step further and establish the method to determine $beta$. From a theoretical perspective, a coherent state is generated by the "displacement operator" $hat(D) ( beta )$ 
$
|beta⟩ & = hat(D) ( beta ) |0⟩   quad "where" med hat(D) ( beta ) equiv exp(beta hat(a)^dagger - beta^(*) hat(a)) .
$ <sq:eq:disp1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:disp1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] In certain ideal situations, the system's time evolution under an external drive can be cast in the form of #ref(<sq:eq:disp1>, supplement: none), which determines the generated coherent state (see also @sec_qm_equation). Furthermore, #ref(<sq:eq:disp1>, supplement: none) provides a powerful theoretical tool for deriving various properties of coherent states. We discuss an alternative method [102,103,104] which is especially useful for studying spin dynamics. A more detailed analysis is presented in the next section


Accounting for a small microwave magnetic field $h cos ( omega_d t ) hat(bold(x))$, the drive Hamiltonian becomes 
$
hat(cal(H))_d & = mu_0 h cos ( omega_d t ) g_e mu_B hat(cal(S))_x = mu_0 h cos ( omega_d t ) g_e mu_B sqrt(frac(N S, 2)) (hat(a) + hat(a)^dagger) equiv epsilon.alt_"rf" ( t ) (hat(a) + hat(a)^dagger) .
$ <sq:eq:Hd>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:Hd")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] Working in the Heisenberg picture, the equation of motion $dot(hat(a)) = i [ hat(cal(H)) + hat(cal(H)_d), hat(a) ]$ yields [#ref(<sq:eq:H1>, supplement: none) and (#ref(<sq:eq:Hd>, supplement: none))] $ frac(d hat(a), d t) & = - i omega_r hat(a) - i epsilon.alt_"rf" ( t ) . $ In steadily oscillating state, we may take the expectation value of the above equation defining $⟨hat(a)⟩ equiv beta$ 
$
frac(d beta, d t) & = - i omega_r beta - i epsilon.alt_"rf" ( t ) - alpha omega_r beta
$ <sq:eq:dyn1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:dyn1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where the last term on the right has been added to account for dissipation. This can be derived rigorously considering a specific bath [103,104], but can also be seen as a phenomenological dissipation term with $alpha$ the Gilbert damping parameter that also enters the LLG phenomenology [53]


#ref(<sq:eq:dyn1>, supplement: none) is a powerful result for multiple reasons. First, its solution expressed in terms of the spin component expectation values is nothing but the linearized LLG equation [53,103,104]. Hence, it serves as a derivation of the linearized classical spin dynamics from a microscopic quantum Hamiltonian. Second, it actually goes beyond the simplified understanding that classical dynamics corresponds to a coherent state. A realistic system, which includes dissipation and nonideal fluctuating drives, is not described by a pure state and thus cannot be a simple coherent state. Nevertheless, #ref(<sq:eq:dyn1>, supplement: none) accounts for such nonidealities. The explicit example of a bosonic dissipative bath is presented in the following @sec_qm_equation.


Finally, assuming that the influence of dissipation and drive nonideality is small, we may approximate the state of our system by a coherent state 
$
|psi ( t )⟩ & = hat(D) ( beta ( t ) ) |0⟩
$ <sq:eq:psit>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:psit")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $beta ( t )$ is determined by #ref(<sq:eq:dyn1>, supplement: none). Furthermore, the quantum fluctuations in the coherent state are the same as in vacuum 
$
Delta cal(S)_x equiv sqrt(⟨beta| hat(cal(S))_x^2 |beta⟩ - (⟨beta| hat(cal(S))_x |beta⟩)^2) = sqrt(⟨0| hat(cal(S))_x^2 |0⟩ - (⟨0| hat(cal(S))_x |0⟩)^2) = sqrt(frac(N S, 2)) = Delta cal(S)_y .
$ <sq:eq:fluc1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("sq:eq:fluc1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] Thus, coherent states correspond to the minimum Heisenberg uncertainty, which is not the case for general states~#footnote[For example, the squeezed states with $theta eq.not 0, pi$ [#ref(<sq:eq:deltasx>, supplement: none) and (#ref(<sq:eq:deltasy>, supplement: none))] bear quantum fluctuations in $cal(S)_(x, y)$ larger than the Heisenberg minimum requirement.]. Moreover, they have an equal magnitude of quantum fluctuations in the two spin components.


#figure(placement: top, image("imgs/phase_space_coherent_state.png", width: 100.0%),
 caption: [Phase space depiction of (a) isotropic ferromagnetic ground state, corresponding to the magnon vacuum, and (b) a magnon coherent state. The shaded region represents the quantum fluctuations due to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle with its area $prop ℏ$. In the coherent state, depicted in (b), the uncertainty region center undergoes precession along a circular trajectory, shown as a dotted curve. For macroscopically large amplitude of the precession, the Heisenberg uncertainty region appears as an infinitesimal point and the classical spin precession picture is recovered.]
) <fig:auto3>


Equipped with the analysis above culminating in #ref(<sq:eq:psit>, supplement: none) and (#ref(<sq:eq:fluc1>, supplement: none)), we portray the spin dynamics, including quantum fluctuations, in its phase space comprised of $cal(S)_x prop Re ( beta )$ and $cal(S)_y prop Im ( beta )$. This has been depicted for the magnon vacuum (ground state of an isotropic ferromagnet) in @fig:auto3(a) and "classical" spin precession in @fig:auto3(b). To summarize, the quantum spin dynamics follows the classical precession expected from the LLG description with a circular minimum Heisenberg uncertainty region around the classical trajectory [103,104]. This picture is an approximation valid when the microwave drive is in an ideal coherent state and the role of dissipation is negligible.




=== Quantum equation of motion
<sec_qm_equation>
In this section, we discuss the quantum dynamics of magnons, now also accounting for anisotropies in our description. Heisenberg equation corresponding to the Hamiltonian (#ref(<hamsse>, supplement: none)) reads 
$
i frac(d hat(a)_i, d t) = [ hat(a)_i, hat(cal(H))^( 2 ) ] = - 2 J S sum_j hat(a)_j + mu_i hat(a)_i + K_x S hat(a)_i^dagger .
$ <HeisenEq>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("HeisenEq")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where the first sum is over neighboring spins of the $i$-th spin. By reformulating these dynamic equations into the form of spin operators, we obtain, $partial_t hat(upright(bold(S)))_i = - gamma ( hat(upright(bold(S)))_i times hat(upright(bold(H)))_(i, "eff") )$, where the effective field is equal to $hat(upright(bold(H)))_(i, "eff") equiv ( 2 J sum_j hat(S)_(j, x) - 2 K_x hat(S)_(i, x) ) upright(bold(e))_x + J sum_j hat(S)_(j, y) upright(bold(e))_y + ( mu_0 g_e mu_B H + 2 K_z S + 2 Z J S ) upright(bold(e))_z$ and $gamma = g_e mu_B$ appears naturally. After taking the mean-field approximation, this equation becomes the same as the classical LLG equation.


When the dissipation of spins is considered, recovering the classical dynamic equations from the quantum mechanical evolution is an ongoing research. The difference between various approaches lies in the way how dissipation is introduced in the quantum models and how the damping of the classical magnetization is recovered [103,104,105,106,107,108]. In principle, all these methods, no matter quantum or classical, should give identical predictions for the low-energy spectrum of SWs. Two known methods to introduce dissipation in a quantum system are the master equation approach based on the Lindblad formalism [109,110], and the Heisenberg-Langevin approach based on the fluctuation-dissipation theorem [111]. Since the latter will be frequently used in this review, we will briefly introduce it here.


 Hamiltonian of the total system including the magnon mode, a bath and their interactions is written as 
$
hat(cal(H)) = hat(cal(H))_s + hat(cal(H))_b + hat(cal(H))_"int"
$ <hambath>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("hambath")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $hat(cal(H))_s, hat(cal(H))_b, hat(cal(H))_"int"$ are respectively the Hamiltonian for the magnon mode, bath and the mutual interaction. Without loss of generality, we shall focus on the FMR mode, and the Hamiltonian for the magnons is $hat(cal(H))_s = omega_r hat(a)^dagger hat(a)$, where $omega_r = omega ( upright(bold(k)) arrow.r 0 )$. The general formalism involving many interacting bosonic modes can be found in Ref. [112]. Next, $hat(cal(H))_b$ is the free Hamiltonian of the bath, which may be formulated as a collection of harmonic oscillators, i.e., $ hat(cal(H)_b) = sum_i omega_i hat(b)_i^dagger hat(b)_i   $ where $hat(b)_i^dagger$ ($hat(b)_i$) is the creation (annihilation) operator for the oscillators which satisfy the bosonic commutation relations $[ hat(b)_i, hat(b)_j^dagger ] = delta_(i j)$. Since the bath usually has a large number of degrees of freedom, we recast the Hamiltonian in the continuum limit as $ hat(cal(H))_b = integral omega hat(b)^dagger ( omega ) hat(b) ( omega ) d omega   $ with the commutations $[ hat(b) ( omega ), hat(b)^dagger ( omega' ) ] = delta ( omega - omega' )$. $hat(cal(H))_"int"$ is the interaction between the magnon modes and the bath 
$
hat(cal(H))_"int" = integral d omega [g ( omega ) hat(a)^dagger hat(b) ( omega ) + g^(*) ( omega ) hat(a) hat(b)^dagger ( omega )] .
$ <couple>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("couple")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where $g ( omega )$ is the magnon-bath coupling strength.


 Hamiltonian (#ref(<hambath>, supplement: none)) leads to the following Heisenberg equation of motion for the magnon mode and bath mode 
#eqgroup((
  [$ frac(d hat(a) ( t ), d t) = - i [ hat(a), hat(cal(H))_s ] - i integral d omega g ( omega ) hat(b) ( omega, t ) $ <hl1>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("hl1")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()]],
  [$ frac(d hat(b) ( omega, t ), d t) = - i omega hat(b) ( omega, t ) - i g^(*) ( omega ) hat(a) ( t ) . $ <hl2>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("hl2")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()]],
), base: 28)
According to #ref(<hl1>, supplement: none), the equation for the bath mode can be explicitly solved as $ hat(b) ( omega, t ) = hat(b) ( omega, t_0 ) e^(- i omega ( t - t_0 )) - i g^(*) ( omega ) integral_(t_0)^t d t' hat(a) ( t' ) e^(- i omega ( t - t' )) . $ By substituting this solution into #ref(<hl1>, supplement: none), we obtain 
$
frac(d hat(a) ( t ), d t) = - i [ hat(a), hat(cal(H))_s ] - i integral d omega g ( omega ) hat(b) ( omega, t_0 ) e^(- i omega ( t - t_0 )) \
-integral d omega g ( omega ) g^(*) ( omega ) integral_(t_0)^t d t' hat(a) ( t' ) e^(- i omega ( t - t' )) .
$ <aeq>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("aeq")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()]


#ref(<aeq>, supplement: none) shows that the state of the magnon at time $t$ depends on its evolution at earlier times, which makes it difficult to handle. The Markov approximation is usually adopted to further simplify the dynamic equations. If the bath is a large system maintained in thermal equilibrium, we expect that it quickly dissipates the information taken from the magnon system and yields no feedback [113]. Mathematically, we now assume that $g ( omega )$ is independent of frequency such that we define $| g ( omega ) |^2 equiv kappa_m \/ pi$, where the frequency-independent coefficient $kappa_m$ denotes the coupling strength between magnons and the bath. Then, #ref(<aeq>, supplement: none) is reduced to 
$
frac(d hat(a) ( t ), d t) = - i [ hat(a), hat(cal(H))_s ] - kappa_m hat(a) ( t ) + sqrt(2 kappa_m) hat(zeta) ( t )
$ <ain>#context text(fill: red, size: 6pt)[LAB#("ain")=#counter(math.equation).get().first()] where the noise operator is defined as $hat(zeta) ( t ) = - i \/ sqrt(2 pi) integral d omega hat(b) ( omega ) e^(- i omega t)$. The damping rate is related to the Gilbert damping parameter as $kappa_m = alpha omega_r$ as used in #ref(<sq:eq:dyn1>, supplement: none). It is straightforward to verify that the noise has zero mean and correlations given by [102] $ chevron.l hat(zeta) ( t ) hat(zeta)^dagger ( t' ) chevron.r = ( n_"th" + 1 ) delta ( t - t' ), quad chevron.l hat(zeta)^dagger ( t ) hat(zeta) ( t' ) chevron.r = n_"th" delta ( t - t' )   $ where $n_"th" = 1 \/ [ "exp" ( omega_r \/ k_B T ) - 1 ]$ with $k_B$ Boltzmann constant and $T$ being the temperature. #ref(<ain>, supplement: none) is also called Heisenberg-Langevin equation. It shows that the evolution of magnons at time $t$ depends only on the information of modes at $t$, and is thus Markovian. The linearity of this equation together with the Gaussian nature of the noise implies that the magnon mode will decay to a stationary Gaussian state.


Let us consider magnon dynamics under a periodic driving, $hat(cal(H))_s = omega_r hat(a)^dagger hat(a) + xi ( hat(a) e^(i omega_d t) + hat(a)^dagger e^(- i omega_d t) )$. In the rotating frame with $cal(R) = exp( - i omega_d hat(a)^dagger hat(a) t )$, the Hamiltonian becomes $hat(cal(H))_s = Delta hat(a)^dagger hat(a) + xi ( hat(a) + hat(a)^dagger )$, where $Delta = omega_r - omega_d$ is the detuning of the magnon mode with respect to the driving. Then the Heisenberg-Langevin equation for the system is $ frac(d hat(a), d t) = - i ( Delta - i kappa_m ) hat(a) - i xi + sqrt(2 kappa_m) hat(zeta) ( t ) . $ By solving this equation for the steady value of $chevron.l a chevron.r$ and $chevron.l a^dagger a chevron.r$, we have $ chevron.l hat(a) chevron.r = - frac(xi, Delta - i kappa_m), quad chevron.l hat(a)^dagger hat(a) chevron.r = frac(xi^2, Delta^2 + kappa_m^2) + n_"th", quad chevron.l hat(a) hat(a) chevron.r = frac(xi^2, ( Delta - i kappa_m )^2) . $ Based on the HP transformation shown above, $hat(cal(S))_x = sqrt(2 N S) ( hat(a) + hat(a)^dagger ) \/ 2, hat(cal(S))_y = sqrt(2 N S) ( hat(a) - hat(a)^dagger ) \/ 2 i$, we can readily evaluate the uncertainty of the spin components $ & Delta cal(S)_x = Delta cal(S)_y = sqrt(frac(N S, 2) (2 n_"th" + 1)) . $ At zero temperature ($n_"th" = 0$), one immediately sees that $ Delta S_x = Delta S_y = sqrt(frac(N S, 2))   $ which recovers the uncertainty relation in #ref(<sq:eq:fluc1>, supplement: none). This implies that the quantum fluctuations of $hat(cal(S))_x$ and $hat(cal(S))_y$ are equal and further satisfy the minimum uncertainty relation, which is a signature of a coherent state [97,98,99]. This indicates that the magnons under strong driving will reproduce the classically observed dynamics [96], and justify the arguments in the discussion of #ref(<sq:eq:fluc1>, supplement: none). At finite temperature, the thermal magnon will play its role, leading to a larger uncertainty of the spin components.


== Quantum entanglement
<sec_en_measure>
 quantum state of a physical system is represented by a state vector in Hilbert space. Depending on the dimension of the Hilbert space, two types of states are classified. (i) If the dimension of the Hilbert space is finite, it is a discrete variable system. The best-known example is the spin-1/2 system or qubit, with only two elements in the Hilbert space ($plus.minus 1 \/ 2$, or up and down, or 0 and 1). Effective qubits have been realized in various solid-state platforms, including superconducting Josephson junctions [114,115], photonic systems [116,117], NV centers [118], silicon-vacancy (SV) centers [119,120], nuclear spins [121], quantum dots [122], trapped ions [123] and topological states of matter [124]. Coherent control of qubits has become the cornerstone of quantum information processing. (ii) If the dimension of the Hilbert space is infinite, this is a continuous variable system. Examples are the position and momentum degrees of freedom of a particle. Besides photons, quasi-particle excitations in solid-state physics are also classified as a continuous variable system, for example, phonons and magnons, because the spectrum of these quasi-particles is continuous. quantum states of quasi-particles, such as the antibunched state, squeezed state, cat state and so on, provide a promising alternative for quantum computing, quantum communication and quantum key distribution [11,13] as we shall see below.


Entanglement corresponds to a nonclassical relation between two distant quantum systems and was first proposed by Einstein, Podolsky, Rosen and Schrödinger in the 1930s Later, such non-local correlations were shown to yield Bell inequalities [128] that have been experimentally tested since 1972 [129]. Nowadays, entanglement of two or more quantum systems has become an indispensable resource for quantum cryptography, quantum teleportation and quantum computation [6]. A fundamental question in this field is how to quantify and manipulate the entanglement of two or more systems. A single answer to this question does not exist. In general, the problem of deciding whether a state is entangled or not is difficult and is considered NP-hard under some reductions [130,131]. Only in low dimensions and for some special cases, one can quantify the entanglement based on a given density matrix of the joint system. Furthermore, the existing criteria for entanglement for discrete and continuous quantum systems are quite different [132], as briefly summarized below. Because it is not our aim to extensively discuss entanglement measures in this review, we will focus on the most important and widely used measures for quantum magnonics.


=== Discrete variable system: qubit
<sec_en_qubit>
#emph[Entanglement entropy.] most popular measure of entanglement for pure states is the von Neumann entanglement entropy [133]. Let us consider a physical system, that has been partitioned into two parts labelled 1 and 2, described by the density matrix $rho_12$. We wish to examine the entanglement between these two parts. von Neumann entanglement entropy is defined as $E equiv - "tr" ( hat(rho)_1 ln hat(rho)_1 )$, where $hat(rho)_1$ is the reduced density matrix of the subsystem, obtained by tracing out the degrees of freedom of the other subsystem, i.e., $hat(rho)_1 = "tr"_2 ( hat(rho)_12 )$. If the two systems are separable, $hat(rho)_1$ is the density matrix of a pure state satisfying $hat(rho)_1^2 = hat(rho)_1$, thus the entropy $E ( hat(rho)_1 )$ vanishes. On the other hand, if the two subsystems are entangled, $hat(rho)_1$ will be a mixed state with finite entropy. magnitude of $E$ directly quantifies the strength of entanglement. Considering two entangled qubits, the maximally entangled states are the four Bell states $ 1 \/ sqrt(2) (| 01 chevron.r + | 10 chevron.r), 1 \/ sqrt(2) (| 00 chevron.r + | 11 chevron.r), 1 \/ sqrt(2) (| 01 chevron.r - | 10 chevron.r), 1 \/ sqrt(2) (| 00 chevron.r - | 11 chevron.r) $ with entropy $ln 2$, which form a maximally entangled basis. For the two-qutrit case, one maximally entangled state is $| phi chevron.r = ( | 11 chevron.r + | 22 chevron.r + | 33 chevron.r ) \/ sqrt(3)$ with entanglement $ln 3$ von Neumann entanglement entropy has become a standard theoretical tool for characterizing, for example, the topological ground states and excitations of quantum spin liquids [135,136]. Its strength as an entanglement measure comes from its broad applicability to pure quantum states - discrete or continuum. Furthermore, by partitioning the system of interest in different ways, it allows for revealing new information about the quantum states [137,138]


#emph[Concurrence.] In most cases, the von Neumann entanglement entropy is a good metric for pure states only. When the two qubits are subject to relaxation and dephasing, the joint density matrix $hat(rho)_12$ represents a mixed state. In general, a mixed state is called separable, if there exist convex weights $p_i$ and product states $hat(rho)_(1, i) ⊗ hat(rho)_(2, i)$ such that $hat(rho)_12 = sum_i p_i hat(rho)_(1, i) ⊗ hat(rho)_(2, i)$ holds. Otherwise the state is called an entangled state [132]


Now the entropy cannot distinguish between classical and quantum correlations and it is no longer a good entanglement measure, because the von Neumann entropy of a subsystem is not zero when the two subsystems are separable [139]. Other metrics for entanglement quantification are now more useful, for example, the entanglement as measured by Wooters' concurrence [140] $ C_12 = max( 0, lambda_1 - lambda_2 - lambda_3 - lambda_4 )   $ where $lambda_1, lambda_2, lambda_3, lambda_4$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of $hat(rho)_12 [ ( hat(sigma)^y ⊗ hat(sigma)^y ) hat(rho)_12^(*) ( hat(sigma)^y ⊗ hat(sigma)^y ) ]$ in the decreasing order, and where $hat(sigma)^nu med ( nu = x, y, z )$ are Pauli matrices and $hat(rho)_12^(*)$ is the complex conjugate of the joint density matrix $hat(rho)_12$. The concurrence is zero if and only if the two qubits are in a separable state. Concurrence has the merit of universal applicability to arbitrary two-qubit states and a larger value of $C_12$ indicates a stronger entanglement. Its generalization to high dimensional pure states was reported in [141]


